<comment datatype='string'>Gram-positive cocci, such as the &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-90964&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Staphylococcaceae&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-1300&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Streptococcaceae&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, and &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-81852&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Enterococcaceae&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt;, possess the &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=PATHWAY&amp;object=PWY-922&quot;&gt;mevalonate pathway&lt;/a&gt; rather than the more commn &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=PATHWAY&amp;object=NONMEVIPP-PWY&quot;&gt;methylerythritol phosphate pathway I&lt;/a&gt;. The pathway genes, which are highly divergent from those of mammals, are believed to have been horizontally transferred from a primitive eukaryotic cell.
&lt;p&gt;
Enterococci uniquely encode a bifunctional protein, encoded by the &lt;i&gt;mvaE&lt;/i&gt; gene, that catalyzes both &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=EC-NUMBER&amp;object=EC-2.3.1.9&quot;&gt;EC 2.3.1.9, acetyl-CoA &lt;em&gt;C&lt;/em&gt;-acetyltransferase&lt;/a&gt; and &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?type=EC-NUMBER&amp;object=EC-1.1.1.34&quot;&gt;EC 1.1.1.34, hydroxymethylglutaryl-CoA reductase (NADPH)&lt;/a&gt; activities. Genetic disruption experiments in &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-1313&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Streptococcus pneumoniae&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt; have shown that the gene is essential [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/10894743&quot;&gt;Wilding00&lt;/a&gt;].
&lt;p&gt;
The &lt;a href=&quot;/gene?orgid=META&amp;id=G-17752&quot;&gt;mvaE&lt;/a&gt; gene was cloned from &lt;a href=&quot;/META/NEW-IMAGE?object=TAX-1351&quot;&gt;&lt;i&gt;Enterococcus faecalis&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/a&gt; with an N-terminal His tag and expressed in Escherichia coli, and the recombinant protein was purified to homogeneity [&lt;a href=&quot;http://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/pubmed/11914342&quot;&gt;Hedl02&lt;/a&gt;]. As predicted, the purified protein catalyzed both the acetoacetyl-CoA thiolase and HMG-CoA reductase reactions.</comment>
